// Title: New Root Certificate Installed Via CertMgr.EXE
// Sigma ID: ff992eac-6449-4c60-8c1d-91c9722a1d48
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: oscd.community, @redcanary, Zach Stanford @svch0st
// Date: 2023-03-05 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: defense-impairment, T1553.004
// Description: Detects execution of "certmgr" with the "add" flag in order to install a new certificate on the
//   system. Adversaries may install a root certificate on a compromised system to avoid warnings
//   when connecting to adversary controlled web servers.
// False positives:
//   - Help Desk or IT may need to manually add a corporate Root CA on occasion. Need to test if GPO push doesn't trigger FP
// References:
//   - https://github.com/redcanaryco/atomic-red-team/blob/f339e7da7d05f6057fdfcdd3742bfcf365fee2a9/atomics/T1553.004/T1553.004.md
//   - https://securelist.com/to-crypt-or-to-mine-that-is-the-question/86307/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_certmgr_certificate_installation.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, microsoft_xdr schema)

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (FolderPath endswith "\\CertMgr.exe" or ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName =~ "CERTMGT.EXE") and (ProcessCommandLine contains "/add" and ProcessCommandLine contains "root")
